+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Handling
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is identified by a client-generated UUID and can be edited by more than one worker from the same starting state — most often because two workers compared field notes and each independently corrected the same visit while offline. The server receives both edits without a reliable signal that one is more authoritative than the other; arrival order reflects network timing, not correctness. The store must turn two or more independently-submitted edits of one visit into a single current record without silently discarding either submission.
+
+Visits mix unrelated and related fields. Correcting an observer name and adding a missed species are unrelated; revising a species identification and its cover estimate are related — the second value depends on the first. The server has no way to infer this relatedness from field names alone, so any mechanism that acts on a sub-visit granularity risks treating a scientifically related pair of edits as independent.
+
+This decision determines what tablet-sync and review-export are built against: the submission envelope tablets construct, the state tablets must retain locally, and the shape of the work coordinators see before Friday's export.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Freedom from silent scientific miscombination — major.** Turns on the HLD tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. A mechanism that combines two submissions into a record neither worker submitted as such must not do so for fields whose values depend on each other, and the server cannot verify relatedness from field names.
+- **Coordinator review workload — major.** Turns on the HLD tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Two coordinators resolve all review work; the volume and complexity of that work must stay within what two people can sustain against ~6,000 visits a season.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden — moderate.** A mechanism that depends on rules distinguishing field types (scalars, observer lists, species entries) must keep those rules correct as the visit schema evolves; incorrect rules reintroduce the miscombination risk above silently.
+- **Export readiness — moderate.** Turns on the HLD goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*. The fraction of a season's visits that can enter the CSV without waiting on a coordinator affects how much of the partner's weekly data is current.
+- **Submission and sync footprint — minor.** What tablets must retain locally and transmit, relevant to the 48-hour offline tenet but not a deciding factor at 6,000 visits/season scale.
+
+A foundational invariant both options already satisfy and that is not a deciding element: neither option may discard an original submission. Persistence keeps every original payload regardless of which option is chosen.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision ID it started from. When two edits name the same starting revision, both are retained as competing complete revisions of the visit; a coordinator resolves the conflict by selecting or editing one. Tablets track the base revision ID of the visit they are editing and submit complete visit payloads referencing it; the submitting worker sees pending competing revisions rather than a silently accepted change. Review and export treat any visit with more than one unresolved revision as held out of the export set, and give coordinators a revision comparison view showing each competing revision in full.
+
+- Freedom from silent miscombination — **strong fit.** No field is ever combined across submissions; a coordinator sees each revision exactly as its submitter wrote it and chooses between or edits them explicitly. Related and unrelated fields are treated identically because nothing is combined automatically.
+- Coordinator review workload — **weak fit.** Every pair of concurrent edits to the same visit becomes review work, including the common case of two workers independently fixing unrelated fields after comparing notes.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **strong fit.** The mechanism is revision tracking and a comparison view; it carries no field-type-specific logic to build or keep correct.
+- Export readiness — **weak fit.** A visit with any unresolved competing revision is withheld from export in full, even where the competing revisions differ in only one unrelated field.
+- Submission and sync footprint — **strong fit.** Tablets submit whole-visit payloads as they otherwise would; no base snapshot diffing is required.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies the base snapshot it started from and a structured changeset against it. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current revision; changes that touch the same field route to review. Tablets must retain base snapshots and produce structured changesets rather than whole-visit payloads. Review and export need field-level provenance (which submission contributed which field, from which base) and a review view scoped to the overlapping fields, plus maintained merge rules covering scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries.
+
+- Freedom from silent miscombination — **weak fit.** Edits to different fields combine without review even when those fields are scientifically related, such as a species identification and its cover estimate landing in different submissions; the server has no field-name-based way to detect this and route it to review instead.
+- Coordinator review workload — **strong fit.** Only genuinely overlapping field edits reach a coordinator; the common case of unrelated-field corrections clears without review.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **weak fit.** Requires merge rules per field type, maintained as the visit schema changes; a rule that is wrong reintroduces the miscombination risk silently, with no review step to catch it.
+- Export readiness — **strong fit.** Most visits clear automatically, maximizing the fraction of the season's visits current in Friday's export.
+- Submission and sync footprint — **weak fit.** Tablets must retain base snapshots and compute structured changesets rather than submitting whole visits.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The deciding element is freedom from silent scientific miscombination. Field-aware merge's weak fit there is not a tuning problem to defer — the server cannot infer field relatedness from names, so any field-disjointness rule is a standing, maintained guess about which fields are scientifically independent, and a wrong guess combines related observations into a record no one submitted without a review step to catch it. Whole-visit revisions keep every exported value traceable to a submission a worker actually made, which is what the HLD's inspectability tenet asks for. This is taken knowingly against the review-workload criterion and the export-readiness goal: review volume will scale with the rate of independent same-visit corrections, which the team already sees when workers compare field notes, and some uncontested visits will wait on a coordinator alongside genuinely conflicting ones.
+
+Committing to this forecloses automatic combination of disjoint edits for the first release and obligates:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** to retain the base revision ID of the visit being edited, submit whole-visit payloads referencing it, and surface pending competing revisions to the submitting worker rather than treating submission as final.
+- **Review-export** to build a revision comparison screen showing competing revisions in full, and to exclude any visit with unresolved competing revisions from the CSV until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+**When to revisit.** If review-queue volume grows beyond what two coordinators can sustain — most plausibly as the survey program or tablet fleet grows beyond its current scale — field-aware merging becomes worth reconsidering, starting from the fields the team is most confident are never scientifically coupled (for example, observer name corrections) rather than merging broadly. That reconsideration should re-run this decision rather than patch around whole-visit revisions piecemeal, since it changes the submission envelope and the review/export contract this decision sets.
